from above the view appears to show a  city unaffected by yemen's civil war  but aidan's coastline tells a different  story  one of fishermen struggling to make ends  meet  mohammed yahya is one of them he  remembers when thousands of fishing  boats ventured onto the red sea coming  back with tuna sardines and lobster  now there are fewer fishermen and fewer  prospects  the price of boats and fishing materials  have gone up  the fighting and insecurity is really  affecting us economically  life in general is more difficult  before the war started seafood was among  yemen's top exports  but with years of fighting its ports and  processing sites have been destroyed  fishermen have been killed and thousands  more displaced  our days are unpredictable it all  depends on supply and demand sometimes  we buy a good catch from the local  fishermen and we make a profit other  days we lose money  [Applause]  most of yemen's 30 million people need  some form of humanitarian aid  buying fish has become a luxury many  can't afford  prices have doubled in some areas so  more fishermen are sending their catch  abroad for higher profits  about 500 000 people worked in the  fishing industry before the war  now it employs less than half  with limited fuel many can't afford  higher petrol prices for their boats and  others can't get out of debt  reports of fishermen who are killed at  sea aren't made public very often but we  know some of them got shot because they  got into restricted waters zones where  fishing is off limits because of the  conflict  but fishermen say the industry though  damaged is still a vital part of the  community and their livelihoods  they hope in time things will improve  for them  and their country  [Applause]  kazia lopez obayan al jazeera
 

